/* Instructor-source objectives kept verbatim; focused clinical-review cautions Nov 17 2026. */
window.VPQ_PHARM_EXAM2_OBJECTIVE_CARDS=[{"id":"exam2-objective-001","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":1,"q":"Most likely type of DM in dogs and cats?","answer":"Dogs: Type 1 (lack of insulin; adult-onset, irreversible, immune-mediated damage to the pancreas). Cats: Type 2 (insulin receptors desensitized, mostly by obesity; 60% of diabetic cats are obese). Exception: cats can get Type 1 from pancreatitis or tumors, and long hyperglycemia kills their beta cells (Type 2 becomes Type 1).","note":"Instructor answer preserved as uploaded. Clinical clarification: most diabetic dogs are insulin-dependent, but not all cases are proven immune-mediated type 1 disease. Most diabetic cats have type-2-like insulin resistance plus impaired beta-cell function; obesity is a risk factor but not universal. Glucotoxicity can worsen beta-cell dysfunction without automatically reclassifying a cat as autoimmune type 1 diabetes. Treat the 60% figure as lecture-specific rather than a universal prevalence.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":1,"why":"Dogs typically have inadequate endogenous insulin production and need replacement; cats frequently combine insulin resistance with beta-cell dysfunction. Glucotoxicity may further suppress pancreatic beta-cell function. Obesity increases feline resistance, but the lecture's classification is a simplification rather than a complete pathologic diagnosis.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-002","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":2,"q":"Two goals of DM treatment in dogs?","answer":"(1) Eliminate the clinical signs and make them happy. (2) Prevent short-term complications: DKA and hypoglycemia. Remission is rare in dogs because the beta cells are already destroyed.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":2,"why":"Insulin replacement restores glucose uptake and limits uncontrolled lipolysis/ketogenesis. Resolution of PU/PD, weight loss and poor activity indicates meaningful clinical improvement, while excessive insulin can cause dangerous hypoglycemia. Canine remission is uncommon because endogenous insulin-secretory capacity is usually limited.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-003","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":3,"q":"Primary goal of DM treatment in cats?","answer":"Remission, most likely in the first 6 months before hyperglycemia kills the beta cells.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":3,"why":"In some newly diagnosed cats, early glycemic control and dietary management reduce glucotoxicity, allowing residual beta cells to recover enough insulin secretion for remission. Remission is a possibility, not a guarantee, and safe glucose control comes before pursuing it.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-004","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":4,"q":"Amorphous vs crystalline insulin?","answer":"Amorphous = individual insulin molecules in a non-coordinated state, absorbed readily (regular insulin). Crystalline = molecules in a coordinated state, which slows absorption from the injection site.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":4,"why":"Amorphous insulin dissolves and is absorbed more rapidly. Larger organized crystals need to dissolve before absorption, slowing release from the injection depot. Formulation and excipients determine the practical time-action profile.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-005","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":5,"q":"How do protamine, zinc and myristic acid change absorption?","answer":"Protamine: excipient protein that binds insulin so it crystallizes (isophane = dilute protamine). Zinc: crystallizes insulin into hexamers, slowing absorption. Myristic acid: attached to the insulin; after the injection it binds albumin, and the insulin leaks slowly off the albumin complex.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":5,"why":"Protamine slows insulin release through protamine-insulin complexes; zinc can stabilize hexamers/crystals that dissolve gradually; myristic acid attached to detemir promotes reversible albumin binding. These are different methods of extending insulin exposure.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-006","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":6,"q":"Short-acting insulin used in vet med, and when?","answer":"Regular insulin (Humulin R), which has no excipients. Used for DKA and other hyperglycemic crises, IV as a constant- rate infusion or IM. Never SQ: dehydrated patients do not absorb it reliably.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":6,"why":"DKA produces dehydration, ketones, acidosis and total-body electrolyte losses. Soluble regular insulin can be titrated intravenously to suppress ketogenesis while fluids and potassium are monitored. Unreliable subcutaneous absorption in severely dehydrated patients explains the lecture caution; avoid treating DKA with a routine long-acting depot alone.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-007","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":7,"q":"Know: starting drugs for dogs","answer":"Intermediate-acting insulins (NPH, Vetsulin) are the starting drugs for dogs. They are not first choices in cats.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":7,"why":"Most diabetic dogs are insulin-dependent, so a reliable intermediate-acting insulin can provide baseline replacement. Dose choice and duration are patient-specific; the lecture's named first choices should be distinguished from later guideline updates.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-008","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":8,"q":"Know: \"Lente\"","answer":"Intermediate-acting insulins are sometimes called Lente (zinc-acetate preparations such as Vetsulin).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":8,"why":"Lente insulin is a zinc-associated suspension with amorphous and crystalline fractions; these dissolve at different rates, prolonging the action compared with regular soluble insulin. The name describes formulation behavior, not a completely separate receptor mechanism.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-009","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":9,"q":"Know: syringe matching","answer":"Match the syringe to the concentration: U40 syringe for 40 U/mL (Vetsulin, PZI) vs U100 syringe for 100 U/mL.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":9,"why":"U-40 contains 40 units/mL and U-100 contains 100 units/mL. A mismatched syringe converts the wrong volume into the intended number of units, potentially causing severe underdosing or hypoglycemia.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-010","lecture":"Lecture 10","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 1)","number":10,"q":"Know: Vetsulin handling","answer":"Vetsulin must be shaken vigorously. All other preparations are only rolled gently.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":10,"why":"A suspension must be mixed enough to distribute insulin evenly before measuring a dose. Follow the actual product label because shaking and rolling instructions differ; generalizing Vetsulin handling to other insulin products risks dosing variability.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-011","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":1,"q":"Name the five long-acting insulins.","answer":"Ultralente, PZI, Glargine, Detemir, Degludec.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":11,"why":"Ultralente and PZI prolong release through formulation changes, glargine precipitates after subcutaneous injection, detemir reversibly binds albumin, and degludec forms multihexamer depots. The same 'long acting' category includes distinct pharmacokinetic mechanisms.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-012","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":2,"q":"Unusual solubility of glargine and what it means for dilution?","answer":"Glargine's pI is 5.4 (insulin 6.7). It is made at pH 4, where it is 95% soluble. At tissue pH 7.4 it is only about 5% soluble, so it precipitates and slowly leaks out. Do not dilute casually, because changing the pH ruins this. Use the manufacturer's specific diluent.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":12,"why":"Glargine is soluble at its acidic formulated pH but forms microprecipitates at near-neutral tissue pH. This slow-release depot depends on the formulation's pH; casual dilution can disrupt the time-action profile.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-013","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":3,"q":"Caveat with detemir in dogs?","answer":"Detemir has 4× lower affinity for the human insulin receptor, so the human product is 4× concentrated. But its affinity for the canine receptor is normal, so the same dose can cause hypoglycemic crises in dogs.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":13,"why":"Detemir's fatty-acid side chain promotes albumin binding and prolonged availability. Dogs can show marked insulin sensitivity to detemir, making unit-for-unit substitution from another insulin unsafe and increasing hypoglycemia risk.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-014","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":4,"q":"Why are clinical signs not enough to avoid hypoglycemia in cats on long- acting insulin?","answer":"Glargine and detemir cause nocturnal nadirs, which is useful because owners are home to see signs. But cats can be hypoglycemic without clinical signs.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":14,"why":"Clinical signs may miss nocturnal or otherwise silent low glucose episodes, especially in cats. A serial curve or continuous glucose monitor can detect nadirs; insulin safety cannot be determined from appetite or activity alone.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-015","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":5,"q":"Current insulin recommendations for dogs and cats?","answer":"Dogs: NPH or Vetsulin first (glargine U300 if once daily is a must). Cats: glargine (best first choice now), detemir or PZI. Start with twice-daily dosing. Dogs are dosed per kg, cats per cat.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":15,"why":"Dogs commonly require insulin replacement throughout life; some cats may regain sufficient endogenous function for remission. Choice among NPH, lente, PZI, glargine and other insulins depends on species, monitoring and patient response; formulation and dose are not interchangeable.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-016","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":6,"q":"Ultimate therapeutic goal for diabetic cats?","answer":"Remission.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":16,"why":"Reducing glucotoxicity and correcting insulin resistance can allow residual feline beta cells to function sufficiently for remission, but recurrence can occur. Stable clinical signs and avoidance of hypoglycemia remain essential treatment outcomes.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-017","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":7,"q":"Why is hypoglycemia especially concerning in pets?","answer":"It is often life-threatening and pets cannot get an appropriate snack on their own. (It may be a risk you accept in cats when remission is the goal.)","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":17,"why":"Severe hypoglycemia deprives the brain of fuel and may cause weakness, seizures or coma. Unlike people, animals cannot reliably recognize an impending low or seek carbohydrate, so client education and monitoring are critical.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-018","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":8,"q":"Somogyi effect: what, how to detect, what to do?","answer":"A rebound from insulin overdose: a rapid fall in glucose triggers glucose release (a hypoglycemia-hyperglycemia roller coaster). Pattern: 1–2 days of good control then several days of poor control. Detect with a blood glucose curve in dogs (stress ruins it in cats; fructosamine avoids the stress). Reduce the dose and re-evaluate in 2–5 days.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":18,"why":"The uploaded guide describes the historical Somogyi rebound hypothesis. Current feline evidence does not reliably support diagnosing a rebound phenomenon from high glucose alone. Confirm glucose nadirs with curve/CGM before adjusting insulin; distinguish source teaching from newer clinical interpretation.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-019","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":9,"q":"Why do acromegaly or sexual \"intactness\" complicate DM treatment?","answer":"Acromegaly: excess somatotropin from a pituitary tumor is diabetogenic (more hepatic gluconeogenesis). Needs up to 40 U of insulin; levels are pulsatile and sporadically drop, causing sudden hypoglycemia. Octreotide, pasireotide and pegvisomant have been tried; usually fatal within a year. Intactness: his slide lists \"reproductive issues\" as an underlying cause of poor control.  ➕    The standard mechanism is insulin resistance driven by reproductive hormones, which is why spaying is part of the plan.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":19,"why":"Growth hormone antagonizes insulin action and can increase hepatic glucose output, causing marked resistance in cats with hypersomatotropism. Reproductive hormones may also modify insulin sensitivity. When these drivers change, previously appropriate insulin doses can become excessive.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-020","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 2)","number":10,"q":"Why can renal disease change DM treatment?","answer":"The kidneys clear insulin (proteolysis), so in a patient with renal disease exogenous insulin lasts longer. This can even help stretch an inexpensive intermediate insulin like NPH in a dog.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":20,"why":"Kidneys contribute to insulin clearance. Reduced kidney function may prolong insulin action, raising hypoglycemia risk; simultaneously appetite and concurrent illness may change insulin requirements. This requires more careful monitoring rather than assuming renal disease will always help control diabetes.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-021","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 3)","number":1,"q":"What is used to treat DKA?","answer":"Fluids and electrolytes first (correct Na⁺, K⁺ and phosphate; insulin can worsen hypokalemia), then regular insulin as a CRI or IM every 4–6 h, never SQ. Dump the first 50–100 mL of the CRI (insulin sticks to the bag and tubing). Lower glucose slowly (50 mg/dL/h) or the Somogyi effect follows. At 250 mg/dL slow the rate. Start a longer-acting insulin once stable and eating.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":21,"why":"Fluid resuscitation improves perfusion, while potassium and phosphate assessment matters because insulin drives potassium into cells. Regular insulin then reduces lipolysis and ketone generation. The guide's fixed sequence, infusion preparation and rate targets are lecture-specific; current ICU protocols must direct actual treatment.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-022","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 3)","number":2,"q":"Know: non-insulin drugs","answer":"Used for (1) cats whose owners refuse insulin but will not euthanize, (2) an adjunct to insulin in refractory dogs, (3) post-remission cats that need to stay on a normal-carb diet. Most need endogenous insulin, so cats respond better.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":22,"why":"Oral antihyperglycemic drugs require different degrees of residual pancreatic insulin function, whereas SGLT2 inhibitors drive urinary glucose excretion independent of insulin. SGLT2 drugs can cause euglycemic DKA and must not be combined casually with insulin; verify approved patient selection and contraindications.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-023","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 3)","number":3,"q":"Dog with Somogyi on Vetsulin: next insulin?","answer":"A longer-duration insulin, PZI. The dose may be right but is released too fast. Use the same number of units and repeat the BG curve in about a week.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":23,"why":"A longer-acting preparation may alter the insulin nadir, but the suspected Somogyi effect must be established rather than inferred from hyperglycemia. Insulin formulations are not automatically exchangeable unit-for-unit; determine a safe new starting dose with monitoring.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-024","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 3)","number":4,"q":"Why are prandial non-insulin drugs not recommended in cats?","answer":"Cats are unpredictable eaters, so drug timing around a meal does not match what they eat.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":24,"why":"Prandial secretagogues depend on predictable food intake to align drug effect and glucose supply. Cats often graze or eat variable meals, increasing the risk of mismatch between insulin stimulation and carbohydrate intake.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-025","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 3)","number":5,"q":"How is insulin used in bovine and equine DM-like diseases?","answer":"Cattle, negative-energy ketosis: propylene glycol (becomes propionic acid, a glucose precursor) plus a corticosteroid; if severe, IV glucose then PZI every other day. Fat cow ketosis (Type 2 DM): NPH twice daily plus IV glucose. Insulin suppresses fatty acid mobilization and increases glucose uptake. Horses (equine metabolic syndrome): not an insulin disease; treat with weight loss, low-carb diet, pre- soaked hay, metformin timed with feeding, and thyroid hormone.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":25,"why":"Ruminant ketosis and equine metabolic syndrome are not identical to canine/feline diabetes. Negative energy balance drives bovine adipose mobilization and ketogenesis; insulin-related equine disease usually involves resistance and diet/weight management. The source regimens are lecture notes, not universally current treatment protocols.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-026","lecture":"Lecture 11","section":"Anti-diabetes drugs (Diabetes 3)","number":6,"q":"What is streptozotocin and when is it used?","answer":"An alkylating anticancer agent toxic to DNA, from Streptomyces in Blue Rapids, Kansas. It resembles glucose and enters islet cells through Glut2 (not Glut4), so it kills beta cells. Used for insulinoma: low dose every 4–6 weeks so some beta cells survive and insulin is not needed.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":26,"why":"Streptozotocin is selectively toxic to pancreatic beta cells after uptake through glucose transport pathways, damaging DNA. Its potential application in insulinoma requires careful oncology oversight because loss of insulin secretion and systemic toxicity are significant risks.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-027","lecture":"Lecture 12","section":"Thyroid drugs","number":1,"q":"How do the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary promote and regulate thyroid hormone synthesis?","answer":"The hypothalamus releases TRH, which makes the anterior pituitary release TSH, which stimulates the thyroid to make and release T3, T4 and calcitonin. TRH-R and TSH-R are GPCRs. TSH also turns on NIS (the iodide transporter) and thyroglobulin synthesis. T3 and T4 (mostly T4) give negative feedback on both TRH and TSH. Somatostatin inhibits TSH.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":27,"why":"The hypothalamus releases TRH, which triggers pituitary TSH secretion. TSH stimulates thyroid follicular hormone synthesis and release. T3, much of it generated intracellularly from T4, feeds back on hypothalamus and pituitary to suppress TRH and TSH. Calcitonin originates from thyroid C cells and is regulated primarily by calcium, not simply as a TSH-controlled follicular hormone.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-028","lecture":"Lecture 12","section":"Thyroid drugs","number":2,"q":"Synthesis and release mechanisms for thyroid hormones?","answer":"Iodide enters the follicle cell through NIS (with Na⁺; perchlorate blocks NIS). Pendrin moves it into the follicle lumen. Thyroperoxidase iodinates tyrosines on thyroglobulin, making MIT and DIT. The iodinated thyroglobulin is taken back into the cell and proteases release the pieces: MIT + DIT = T3; DIT + DIT = T4. They are released to plasma, mostly T4. In target cells T4 is mono-deiodinated to T3, which binds the T3 receptor with the RXR co-receptor.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":28,"why":"NIS moves iodide across the basolateral membrane; pendrin helps transfer it apically; thyroid peroxidase organifies iodide and couples MIT/DIT residues on thyroglobulin. Endocytosis and proteolysis release T3/T4. Methimazole blocks synthesis, not immediate release of stored hormones.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-029","lecture":"Lecture 12","section":"Thyroid drugs","number":3,"q":"Name three drugs that can cause pseudohypothyroidism.","answer":"Glucocorticoids (low T4, normal TSH; adjust the dose if possible), phenobarbital (low T4, possible TSH rise; no worry about true hypothyroidism), sulfonamide antibiotics (T3 and T4 low, TSH high; true hypothyroidism if used long-term). His slide also lists carprofen. Other causes: sick euthyroid syndrome, and sighthounds and northern breeds.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":29,"why":"Glucocorticoids, phenobarbital and some sulfonamides can change thyroid testing or actual thyroid function. A low total T4 is not diagnostic of primary hypothyroidism in an ill or medicated dog; interpret signs, free T4 and TSH together.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-030","lecture":"Lecture 12","section":"Thyroid drugs","number":4,"q":"Treatment of hypothyroidism in dogs, cats and horses?","answer":"Levothyroxine (T4) in all three, given on an empty stomach in dogs. Dogs: the most common species; T4 is the gold standard. T3 is not used clinically because only T4 gives negative feedback. Cats: rare; iatrogenic (after treating hyperthyroidism) or cretinism. Horses: rare, verify low T4 with high TSH first; T4 is also used to speed weight loss in EMS. Poor response: outdated tablets, or switching brands (different bioavailability).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":30,"why":"Levothyroxine replaces circulating T4, which peripheral tissues can convert to active T3, restoring metabolic signaling. Therapy and monitoring depend on true thyroid dysfunction; suspected hypothyroidism in horses requires careful differential diagnosis.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-031","lecture":"Lecture 12","section":"Thyroid drugs","number":5,"q":"Treatments for feline hyperthyroidism?","answer":"Four options: (1) Y/D low-iodine diet for non-severe cases (over 80% remission, poor palatability, cheapest). (2) Methimazole, which inhibits thyroperoxidase so less MIT and DIT; cheap, transdermal form exists, good for geriatric cats; monitor CBC, T4, platelets, BUN and creatinine, liver enzymes. PTU and carbimazole work the same but have more side effects. (3) I¹³¹, the most effective and most expensive ($2,200–$2,700); start methimazole first to stabilize and check metabolic, CV and renal function; stop methimazole and Y/D 10–14 days before; radiation precautions after; may need T4 afterward. (4) Thyroidectomy, pretreat with methimazole; risks are ectopic tissue left behind, needing T4, and parathyroid damage.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":31,"why":"Iodine restriction reduces substrate for synthesis; methimazole blocks TPO-mediated organification/coupling; I-131 destroys functional thyroid tissue; surgery removes it. They differ in reversibility, renal implications, safety and suitability for individual cats.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-032","lecture":"Lecture 12","section":"Thyroid drugs","number":6,"q":"Treatment of hypoparathyroidism and milk fever?","answer":"Hypoparathyroidism (after thyroidectomy or innate): oral calcium lactate or carbonate plus calcitriol. Milk fever: IV calcium borogluconate; if the cow will not rise once calcium is normal, tripelennamine or hypertonic saline. Prevention: anionic salts (Cl and S) in the dry period, which sequester dietary calcium and so activate PTH.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":32,"why":"Calcium and calcitriol replace impaired PTH-dependent calcium regulation in hypoparathyroidism. Around calving, dairy cows can fail to adapt quickly to the huge calcium drain into milk; IV calcium addresses symptomatic hypocalcemia while diet/acid-base strategy influences prevention.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-033","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":1,"q":"Major NT of the PNS?","answer":"Acetylcholine (at both synapses).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":33,"why":"Most parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release acetylcholine, which acts on muscarinic receptors at target organs. Preganglionic fibers use acetylcholine at nicotinic ganglionic receptors, so identify which synapse the question asks about.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-034","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":2,"q":"Major effector NTs of the SNS at the organ?","answer":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine (adrenergic).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":34,"why":"Most sympathetic postganglionic effector fibers release norepinephrine, though important exceptions include cholinergic sweat gland innervation and adrenal medullary epinephrine release. Adrenergic receptor subtype determines the organ effect.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-035","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":3,"q":"NT common to SNS and PNS?","answer":"Acetylcholine (ganglionic synapse in both; also the SNS nerve to the adrenal medulla chromaffin cells, the only ANS nerve with no ganglion).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":35,"why":"Both sympathetic and parasympathetic preganglionic neurons release acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors in autonomic ganglia. This shared transmitter does not mean their effector organs respond identically.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-036","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":4,"q":"Seven major ANS receptors?","answer":"Nicotinic (ion channel; ganglia and NMJ), muscarinic (GPCR at the effector), and adrenergic α1, α2, β1, β2, β3 (all GPCRs). α2 is covered in neuropharmacology.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":36,"why":"Adrenergic alpha-1/alpha-2/beta-1/beta-2/beta-3 receptors and cholinergic muscarinic/nicotinic receptors differ in tissue distribution and signaling. Learn receptor effect first, then predict how agonists and antagonists reverse it.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-037","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":5,"q":"To prevent urination: which SNS receptor to activate and which PNS receptor to inhibit?","answer":"Activate α1 (sphincter contraction; β1 also relaxes the bladder wall) and inhibit the muscarinic receptor. Drugs: phenylpropanolamine (α1 and β1 agonist, some CV effects); oxybutynin (muscarinic antagonist, semi-selective for bladder, fewer CV but more GI effects).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":37,"why":"Alpha-1 adrenergic activation tightens urethral outlet smooth muscle; muscarinic blockade decreases bladder detrusor contraction. Together they favor storage but can precipitate retention in an obstructed patient.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-038","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":6,"q":"To promote urination: which PNS receptor to activate and which SNS receptor to inhibit?","answer":"Activate the muscarinic receptor and inhibit α1. Drugs: bethanechol (muscarinic agonist), prazosin, tamsulosin, phenoxybenzamine (α1 antagonists).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":38,"why":"Muscarinic activation contracts detrusor muscle and alpha-1 antagonism relaxes urethral smooth muscle. Effective urination requires both a contracting bladder and low outlet resistance; rule out obstruction first.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-039","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":7,"q":"To promote mydriasis: SNS receptor to activate, PNS receptor to inhibit?","answer":"Activate α1 and inhibit the muscarinic receptor. Drugs: phenylephrine, tropicamide, atropine.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":39,"why":"Alpha-1 stimulation contracts iris radial dilator muscle; muscarinic inhibition prevents sphincter contraction. Both promote mydriasis but differ in cycloplegic and systemic effects.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-040","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":8,"q":"To promote miosis: PNS receptor to activate, SNS receptor to inhibit?","answer":"Activate the muscarinic receptor and inhibit α1. Drugs: pilocarpine, demecarium bromide.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":40,"why":"Muscarinic stimulation contracts iris sphincter muscle and produces miosis. Adrenergic blockade can reduce dilator tone, but drug choice depends on glaucoma type and lens/uveal health.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-041","lecture":"Lecture 13","section":"Autonomic pharmacology","number":9,"q":"MOAs and uses of the listed drugs","answer":"Phenylpropanolamine: non-selective α1 and β1 agonist; urine retention (incontinence); some CV side effects. Oxybutynin: muscarinic antagonist, bladder-selective; urine retention; fewer CV, more GI effects. Prazosin: non- selective α1 antagonist; promotes urination; some CV effects. Tamsulosin: selective α1 antagonist; urethrospasm from urethritis or urethroliths. Phenoxybenzamine: irreversible α1 antagonist; mainly for pheochromocytoma hypertension (urination is a side effect). Bethanechol: muscarinic agonist; promotes urination; contraindicated if the bladder wall is compromised or after urethral obstruction. Tropicamide: muscarinic antagonist; short-acting mydriatic for eye exams. Atropine: muscarinic antagonist; long-acting; ocular comfort and less posterior synechia in uveitis; very long half-life in horses (colic risk); use ointment in cats (drops cause hypersalivation); contraindicated in glaucoma and KCS. Phenylephrine: α1 agonist; mydriasis, sometimes combined with tropicamide or atropine. Pilocarpine and demecarium bromide: muscarinic agonists; miotics for glaucoma (unless secondary to lens luxation) and lens instability. Succinylcholine: activates the nicotinic receptor at the NMJ and is not broken down by AChE, so it overstimulates it; flaccid paralysis, then diaphragm paralysis at higher doses; used for euthanasia in wildlife and equine castration.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":41,"why":"The lecture lists drug-receptor pairs across urinary, eye and autonomic toxicity topics. Clinical recall is stronger when organized by receptor: alpha-1 closes the urethra/dilates pupils; muscarinic contracts detrusor/constricts pupil; antagonists reverse these actions.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-042","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":1,"q":"Mechanistic goal of anti-CHF drugs?","answer":"Raise intra-myocytic calcium so contractility improves.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":42,"why":"Contractile force depends on actin-myosin interaction enabled by intracellular calcium. Positive inotropes raise effective calcium signaling or increase myofilament sensitivity; not all are appropriate for chronic CHF.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-043","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":2,"q":"MOAs of the five anti-CHF drugs?","answer":"Digoxin: inhibits the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, so Na⁺ builds up, the Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger runs in reverse, and Ca²⁺ rises. Dopamine: D1 agonist, so adenylyl cyclase makes cAMP, which mobilizes Ca²⁺. Dobutamine: β1 agonist, same cAMP path. Amrinone: PDE3 inhibitor, so cAMP rises. Pimobendan: PDE3 inhibitor plus calcium sensitizer (binds troponin C and raises its affinity for Ca²⁺).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":43,"why":"Digoxin raises calcium indirectly by inhibiting Na+/K+-ATPase; dopamine and dobutamine use adrenergic/cAMP pathways; amrinone inhibits PDE3; pimobendan sensitizes troponin C to calcium and inhibits PDE3. Different mechanisms imply different adverse effects and clinical roles.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-044","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":3,"q":"Which anti-CHF drug is also a vasodilator?","answer":"Pimobendan (an \"inodilator\"); it opens a K⁺ channel in vascular smooth muscle. Approved only in dogs.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":44,"why":"Pimobendan increases myocardial contractility while relaxing vascular smooth muscle, lowering afterload. It is an inodilator, unlike a purely positive inotrope.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-045","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":4,"q":"Which is more likely to be toxic if the patient is hypokalemic?","answer":"Digoxin. Many diuretics cause hypokalemia, and diuretics are often given with digoxin.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":45,"why":"Low potassium increases digoxin interaction with Na+/K+-ATPase, raising toxicity risk. Diuretics that deplete potassium can therefore make digoxin-associated arrhythmias more likely.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-046","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":5,"q":"Common toxicity of all anti-CHF drugs?","answer":"Arrhythmias. For digoxin also AV block, PVCs, pulsus bigeminy, Vfib, GI signs. Treat by stopping the drug, treating the arrhythmia, IV fluids for renal clearance, and careful K⁺ if hypokalemic.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":46,"why":"Excess inotropic stimulation or altered electrophysiology can provoke arrhythmias. The exact toxicity differs by drug, so 'arrhythmia' is an overarching caution rather than the only adverse effect.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-047","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":6,"q":"Two drugs for ventricular ectopic beats, and their MOA?","answer":"Lidocaine and mexiletine: Na⁺ channel blockers that bind rapidly and selectively to refractory channels. Lidocaine is IV for acute ventricular arrhythmias; mexiletine is an oral syrup for long-term use. Not for atrial problems. ⚠ His Class 4 slide also lists premature ectopic beats (esp. PVCs) as the main use of verapamil and diltiazem.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":47,"why":"Lidocaine and mexiletine are Class IB sodium-channel blockers used for selected ventricular arrhythmias. Lidocaine is often used IV acutely; mexiletine can be administered orally. They do not treat all arrhythmias.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-048","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":7,"q":"Which anti-arrhythmic causes corneal deposits, and its MOA?","answer":"Amiodarone (Class 3): blocks K⁺ and Ca²⁺ channels and prolongs the refractory period. Also pulmonary fibrosis and GI upset; contraindicated if bradycardic.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":48,"why":"Amiodarone has several channel effects, notably potassium-channel blockade that prolongs refractoriness; tissue deposition can cause corneal changes and other organ toxicity. Monitor rhythm, liver and additional relevant adverse effects.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-049","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":8,"q":"Two anti-arrhythmics that selectively bind refractory Na⁺ channels?","answer":"Lidocaine and mexiletine.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":49,"why":"Class IB drugs such as lidocaine and mexiletine preferentially affect inactivated sodium channels in depolarized or ischemic myocardium, limiting ectopic ventricular activity.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-050","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":9,"q":"Preferred drug for long-term oral treatment of V-tach and ventricular arrhythmias, and its MOA?","answer":"Procainamide: Class I Na⁺ channel blocker with moderate association and dissociation, non-selective for open vs refractory channels. Halve the dose with renal disease. Contraindicated for conduction block.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":50,"why":"Procainamide is a Class IA sodium-channel blocker that slows conduction and prolongs repolarization. It is one possible oral choice but should not be described as universally preferred over all contemporary alternatives.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-051","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":10,"q":"Cat with hyperthyroid arrhythmia: which drug, MOA?","answer":"Propranolol: a non-selective β blocker; blocking β1 lowers excitation and rate. Raise the dose slowly to avoid bronchospasm (anti-β2).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":51,"why":"Hyperthyroidism increases cardiac adrenergic responsiveness and may cause tachyarrhythmia. A beta blocker reduces adrenergic heart-rate effects but does not fix thyroid-hormone excess.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-052","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":11,"q":"Cat with hyperthyroid hypertension without arrhythmia: which drug, MOA?","answer":"Amlodipine: Ca²⁺ channel blocker (same MOA as diltiazem), so less Ca²⁺ in vascular smooth muscle, vasodilation, lower pressure. Front-line for feline hypertension.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":52,"why":"Amlodipine blocks vascular L-type calcium channels, reducing systemic vascular resistance. It targets hypertension itself, while treating hyperthyroidism addresses a contributing endocrine cause.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-053","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":12,"q":"Hydralazine MOA, why emergency-only, and the other emergency drug?","answer":"Hydralazine: blocks Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, increases smooth muscle sensitivity to NO, and opens a K⁺ channel. Emergency-only because it can drop pressure enough to trigger the baroreflex and tachycardia with extended use. Sodium nitroprusside donates NO, activating the NANC pathway, so vasodilation.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":53,"why":"Hydralazine reduces arterial resistance, but excessive vasodilation can provoke hypotension and baroreflex-mediated tachycardia. Sodium nitroprusside releases nitric oxide and is also a titratable emergency vasodilator.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-054","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":13,"q":"How does telmisartan lower blood pressure?","answer":"It antagonizes the angiotensin II (AT1) receptor, which ultimately raises NO and causes vasodilation. FDA-approved for feline hypertension; also reduces proteinuria.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":54,"why":"Telmisartan blocks angiotensin II type-1 receptors, opposing vasoconstriction and aldosterone-related sodium retention. In cats it can also reduce proteinuria; clinical monitoring includes blood pressure and renal parameters.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-055","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":14,"q":"Two ACE inhibitors, how they treat hypertension, how they assist anti-CHF drugs?","answer":"Enalapril and benazepril. They block ACE (angiotensin I to II), so less AT1 activation and vasodilation. Less angiotensin II means less aldosterone, so diuresis and a lower fluid load, which helps CHF. They offset the rise in blood pressure that anti- CHF drugs cause by increasing cardiac output.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":55,"why":"ACE inhibitors reduce angiotensin II formation, lowering vascular resistance and aldosterone effects. Enalapril and benazepril may support CHF therapy but require kidney, potassium and blood-pressure monitoring.","whyProvenance":"Added physiology/clinical interpretation; separate from original professor-guide answer; cross-checked against 51-page Questions/Answers PDF and 142-page study guide."},{"id":"exam2-objective-056","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":15,"q":"Drug for pulmonary hypertension and MOA?","answer":"Sildenafil, a PDE5 inhibitor. More cGMP, so PKG opens a Ca²⁺-lowering channel and vascular smooth muscle stays relaxed.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":56,"why":"Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, raising cGMP. PDE5 normally degrades cGMP; sildenafil prolongs the cGMP/PKG relaxation signal, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance. It does not directly fix every cause of pulmonary hypertension, so underlying disease and systemic blood pressure matter.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-057","lecture":"Lecture 14","section":"Cardiovascular pharmacology","number":16,"q":"Mainstay drug for cardiac resuscitation?","answer":"Epinephrine (adrenaline). Atropine, bicarbonate, calcium, magnesium and fibrinolytics only in specific circumstances.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":57,"why":"During cardiac arrest, epinephrine's alpha-1 vasoconstriction raises aortic diastolic pressure, helping maintain coronary and cerebral perfusion during compressions. Beta effects can increase myocardial excitability but may also provoke arrhythmias. High-quality CPR, ventilation and rhythm-specific defibrillation remain essential; atropine or calcium are not universal arrest medications.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-058","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":1,"q":"Three facets controlling airway tone, and how two can be manipulated?","answer":"Parasympathetic (ACh at M3 receptors; baseline mild bronchoconstriction), sympathetic (β2 receptors cause bronchodilation; α receptors constrict), and NANC (nitric oxide via vagal fibers; bronchodilation, but hard to manipulate). Bronchodilate by activating β2 (β agonists) or blocking muscarinic receptors (anticholinergics).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":58,"why":"Airway smooth muscle contracts when vagal acetylcholine activates M3/Gq pathways and raises intracellular calcium. Beta-2/Gs signaling increases cAMP and relaxes smooth muscle, while inhibitory NANC pathways can release nitric oxide. Blocking M3 with ipratropium or stimulating beta-2 with albuterol targets separate mechanisms; neither directly cures airway inflammation.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-059","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":2,"q":"Types, names and selectivities of β agonists?","answer":"Non-selective (β1 and β2): epinephrine, ephedrine, isoproterenol; α activity adds vasoconstriction and hypertension. Selective β2: terbutaline (oral or parenteral; cat asthma), albuterol (oral, aerosol; also lowers serum K⁺, useful for urinary obstruction), clenbuterol (equine syrup, lasts 6–8 h). Isoetharine is nebulized and not β2-selective; pirbuterol, salmeterol, formoterol are inhaled.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":59,"why":"Beta-2 stimulation increases cAMP and lowers airway smooth muscle contractile tone; some beta-2 drugs also shift potassium into cells via Na+/K+-ATPase. Nonselective agonists activate cardiac beta-1 and sometimes vascular alpha receptors, causing tachycardia or hypertension. Selectivity is dose-dependent, so a 'selective' bronchodilator may still cause systemic effects.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-060","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":3,"q":"Methylxanthines and how they bronchodilate?","answer":"Theophylline and aminophylline (aminophylline is used in small animals). They inhibit phosphodiesterase, so cAMP is not broken down; they also interfere with Ca²⁺ mobilization and block adenosine receptors. Side effects: CNS excitation, vomiting, diuresis, tachycardia; slow-release products preferred.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":60,"why":"Theophylline and aminophylline increase cyclic-nucleotide signaling in part through PDE inhibition and also antagonize adenosine receptors. This can relax bronchi but also stimulates the CNS and heart, explaining agitation, vomiting and tachycardia. Their narrower margin and variable metabolism mean they are not simply interchangeable with inhaled beta-2 agonists.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-061","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":4,"q":"Issues with clenbuterol and zilpaterol in horses and cattle?","answer":"Clenbuterol: approved in horses (Ventipulmin); high doses activate β1 (tachycardia, excitement, sweating). Abused in racehorses (ARCI class 3) though it gives a healthy horse no cardiopulmonary advantage; chronic use repartitions toward muscle. Banned as a feed additive. Zilpaterol (Zilmax): cattle growth promoter withdrawn voluntarily because of health problems including lameness; highly toxic to horses.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":61,"why":"Clenbuterol relaxes equine airway smooth muscle via beta-2 receptors but higher exposures can also stimulate cardiovascular beta receptors and affect skeletal-muscle metabolism. Zilpaterol alters adrenergic signaling and nutrient partitioning in cattle; adverse musculoskeletal and food-animal regulatory issues are separate from therapeutic bronchodilation. Racing status, labeled species and current rules must be checked rather than relying on a fixed historical classification.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-062","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":5,"q":"Recognize an anticholinergic bronchodilator","answer":"Atropine (acutely dyspneic animal; tachycardia, ileus, less mucociliary clearance), ipratropium (better bronchodilator, does not cross the blood- brain barrier, poorly absorbed from aerosol), glycopyrrolate (slower onset, longer half-life).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":62,"why":"Atropine, ipratropium and glycopyrrolate compete with acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, removing vagally mediated airway constriction. Inhaled quaternary ipratropium has limited systemic and CNS exposure; systemic antimuscarinics can cause tachycardia, reduced GI motility and dry secretions. These drugs address bronchospasm, not all causes of dyspnea.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-063","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":6,"q":"Recognize expectorants, peripheral and central antitussives","answer":"Expectorant: potassium iodide (guaifenesin and ambroxol also ease coughing up secretions). Peripheral: expectorants, and bronchodilators like ephedrine and theophylline that relieve irritant receptor stimulation. Central narcotic: codeine, hydrocodone, butorphanol (sedation, constipation). Central non-narcotic: dextromethorphan. Do not suppress a cough unless it is excessive or debilitating.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":63,"why":"A cough expels irritants and secretions, so suppressing a productive cough can impair pulmonary defense. Expectorants/mucolytics change secretion handling, bronchodilators relieve cough triggers caused by narrowed airways, and centrally acting opioid or nonopioid drugs reduce cough-reflex signaling. Select treatment based on cough character and cause rather than suppressing every cough.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-064","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":7,"q":"Actions and uses of doxapram and thioredoxin","answer":"Doxapram: IV respiratory stimulant; activates carotid body chemoreceptors, which stimulate the brainstem respiratory center; used peri-surgically and can reverse opioid respiratory depression. Thioredoxin: protein disulfide reductase that breaks disulfide links in mucins (excess mucus) and activates β- defensins; a nebulized lung-specific analog is a potential treatment for BRD and heaves.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":64,"why":"Doxapram stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors and respiratory drive, but it does not relieve an obstructed airway or replace assisted ventilation; an opioid antagonist may be needed for opioid-related depression. Thioredoxin can reduce protein disulfide bonds, potentially changing mucus properties; lung-targeted use described in the guide is investigational rather than established routine treatment.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-065","lecture":"Lecture 15","section":"Respiratory pharmacology","number":8,"q":"Which antibiotics are good for respiratory infections?","answer":"BRD metaphylaxis: tulathromycin, gamithromycin, tildipirosin, tilmicosin. BRD treatment: florfenicol ± flunixin, ceftiofur, enrofloxacin, danofloxacin, pradofloxacin, tulathromycin + ketoprofen. Dairy cattle, calves, swine, horses: ceftiofur. Dogs and cats: amoxicillin + clavulanate.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":65,"why":"Choice of respiratory antimicrobials depends on likely pathogens, drug susceptibility, tissue penetration and species/legal labeling. Macrolides, florfenicol, ceftiofur and fluoroquinolones have different spectra, resistance risks and food-animal restrictions. The lecture's memorization list is a starting framework, not a universal prescription for every cattle, horse, dog or cat respiratory infection.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-066","lecture":"Lecture 16","section":"Renal pharmacology (diuretics)","number":1,"q":"When to use diuretics?","answer":"To make a large volume of dilute urine and shrink extracellular fluid volume. Used where extra ECF is harmful: CHF (pulmonary edema and dyspnea), hepatic disease (ascites, low protein edema), renal disease with Na⁺ retention, equine exercise-induced pulmonary hemorrhage. Oliguric acute renal failure is only a palliative attempt and may do harm.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":66,"why":"Natriuresis lowers extracellular-fluid volume, reducing venous congestion and pulmonary or peripheral edema. It helps when fluid overload causes signs, but can worsen perfusion in an already dehydrated patient. Diuretics do not repair injured nephrons or reliably improve survival in oliguric acute kidney injury just by producing urine.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-067","lecture":"Lecture 16","section":"Renal pharmacology (diuretics)","number":2,"q":"Where do the different classes work, and what does that mean for strengths and weaknesses?","answer":"Proximal tubule: osmotic diuretics (mannitol) and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. Thick ascending limb: loop diuretics (NKCC2). Late distal tubule: thiazides (Na-Cl symporter). Collecting duct: spironolactone, K⁺-sparing diuretics, aquaretics. Most water (70%) is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, but the nephron downstream can compensate; later sites give weaker diuresis. Knowing the site explains each drug's limits and why drugs are combined (sequential blockade).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":67,"why":"Loop drugs inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, a high-capacity salt-reabsorbing segment essential to the medullary gradient. Thiazides inhibit distal NCC; spironolactone blocks aldosterone signaling and amiloride blocks ENaC in later segments. Downstream compensation limits each class, while sequential blockade can enhance sodium excretion but increases adverse electrolyte risk.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-068","lecture":"Lecture 16","section":"Renal pharmacology (diuretics)","number":3,"q":"Adverse effects of diuretics as a class, and why?","answer":"Hypovolemia (less Na⁺ and water retained), pre-renal azotemia (low volume lowers renal blood flow, BUN rises), electrolyte loss (Na⁺, Cl⁻, K⁺, and indirectly Mg²⁺ and Ca²⁺), metabolic alkalosis (preferential Cl⁻ loss). Hypokalemia: extra Na⁺ reaches the late distal tubule and eNaC swaps it for K⁺. Manage by monitoring (owner checks CRT; bloodwork at 24–48 h and 7–14 days), supplementing fluids and electrolytes, and using the lowest effective dose.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":68,"why":"Water and sodium loss can lower effective circulating volume and kidney perfusion, causing prerenal azotemia. Distal sodium delivery promotes potassium and hydrogen-ion secretion, contributing to hypokalemic, hypochloremic alkalosis with many loop/thiazide agents; potassium-sparing agents instead risk hyperkalemia. Monitoring electrolytes, body weight, hydration and renal values matters more than assuming a single universal recheck interval.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-069","lecture":"Lecture 16","section":"Renal pharmacology (diuretics)","number":4,"q":"Why is furosemide such an effective diuretic?","answer":"It inhibits NKCC2 (Na⁺/K⁺/2Cl⁻) in the thick ascending limb, so solutes stay in the lumen and water follows. Potent, high ceiling, safe, fast (30 min IV, 2 h oral), cheap, many routes, works in all five major species. Downsides: short duration (4–5 h), reversible ototoxicity, and a \"braking\" effect (volume loss triggers RAAS). Uses: CHF, equine EIPH, hypertension, edema, and preparturient udder edema (drug of choice since it will not induce parturition; 48 h milk withhold).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":69,"why":"Furosemide blocks NKCC2, preventing sodium, potassium and chloride reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and reducing the renal concentrating gradient. It rapidly mobilizes excess fluid in CHF, but excessive diuresis decreases renal perfusion. Hypokalemia, hypochloremia, ototoxicity and compensatory RAAS activation explain important monitoring and the 'braking' effect. Withdrawal periods cited in the guide are product- and jurisdiction-specific.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-070","lecture":"Lecture 16","section":"Renal pharmacology (diuretics)","number":5,"q":"Disadvantages of furosemide and why torsemide might be better?","answer":"Torsemide (approved in dogs): more diuresis per mg, longer-acting, slower onset so smoother diuresis, and approved with pimobendan, spironolactone and an ACE inhibitor for CHF pulmonary edema. Catch: potency makes dosing hard to control (over- or under-diuresis).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":70,"why":"Torsemide inhibits the same NKCC2 transporter as furosemide but is generally more potent per milligram and longer-acting. More sustained sodium loss may help difficult congestion, yet the same mechanism can over-deplete fluid and electrolytes. Conversion is not milligram-for-milligram; follow product-specific and patient-specific monitoring.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-071","lecture":"Lecture 16","section":"Renal pharmacology (diuretics)","number":6,"q":"Spironolactone MOA, why no K⁺ wasting, and why its RAAS effect helps CHF?","answer":"It blocks the mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) receptor in the late distal tubule and collecting duct, so Na⁺ (and water) stay in the lumen and K⁺ is kept. It is a weak diuretic alone but pairs with loop diuretics to limit K⁺ loss (sequential blockade). It also limits aldosterone- driven inflammation and fibrosis, and stops aldosterone breakthrough when ACE inhibition is incomplete, so it controls ECF better with an ACE inhibitor. Cautions: hyperkalemia, raises digoxin half-life, not for food animals, male reproductive effects (anti-androgen).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":71,"why":"Blocking mineralocorticoid receptors reduces aldosterone-driven ENaC expression and distal potassium secretion, modestly reducing sodium retention while sparing potassium. Mineralocorticoid blockade can also blunt fibrotic and remodeling signals implicated in CHF. Hyperkalemia risk increases with kidney disease or other RAAS-blocking drugs; spironolactone is not an adequate rapid rescue diuretic alone.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-072","lecture":"Lecture 16","section":"Renal pharmacology (diuretics)","number":7,"q":"Lesser-used diuretics: MOA, uses, adverse effects","answer":"Osmotic (mannitol): inert glucose-like sugar that holds water in the proximal tubule; weak, IV only; emergency for high intraocular or intracranial pressure; adverse: rapid volume expansion and hypertension. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide): block Na⁺ retention path in the proximal tubule; glaucoma, edema, metabolic alkalosis; adverse: metabolic acidosis (avoid in pulmonary disease), uroliths from alkaline urine, hypokalemia. Thiazides: block the Na-Cl symporter in the late distal tubule; second choice behind loops for CHF; hydrochlorothiazide for cattle udder edema (72 h withhold); adverse: more Ca²⁺ reabsorption, impair anticoagulants and insulin, blunted by NSAIDs. K⁺- sparing (triamterene, amiloride): block eNaC; weak, seldom used alone, spironolactone is better; adverse: hyperkalemia.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":72,"why":"Mannitol holds water in tubular fluid and can initially expand vascular volume; acetazolamide lowers bicarbonate reabsorption, explaining both diuresis and possible metabolic acidosis. Thiazides block NCC and tend to increase calcium reabsorption, whereas amiloride/triamterene block ENaC and spare potassium. These different mechanisms explain why one drug cannot replace another indiscriminately. Historical cattle withdrawal figures must be verified against labels and regulations.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-073","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":1,"q":"MOAs of the three main anti-emetics?","answer":"Metoclopramide: D2 dopamine receptor antagonist. Maropitant: NK1 receptor antagonist (blocks visceral input to the CTZ and vestibular input). Ondansetron: 5-HT3 antagonist (also blocks serotonin release and vagal efferents).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":73,"why":"Metoclopramide blocks D2 receptors at emetic signaling sites and enhances selected upper-GI motility. Maropitant antagonizes NK1 receptors for substance P in central emetic pathways, whereas ondansetron blocks 5-HT3 signaling from vagal afferents and the CNS. These pathways overlap but are not identical; the best drug depends on the trigger and patient.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-074","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":2,"q":"Which stimulates gastric emptying, which is contraindicated with liver disease, which is reserved for refractory cases?","answer":"Metoclopramide stimulates gastric emptying (and is contraindicated with GI obstruction). Maropitant: caution with hepatic problems. Ondansetron: most potent, reserved for severe or refractory emesis.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":74,"why":"Metoclopramide increases upper-GI motility, which can be dangerous against a mechanical obstruction. Maropitant is metabolized hepatically, so significant liver disease warrants caution rather than an absolute universal prohibition. Ondansetron's 5-HT3 blockade may help chemotherapy-associated or refractory emesis, but 'most potent' is context dependent.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-075","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":3,"q":"Three pro-emesis drugs for dogs, two for cats, and their MOAs?","answer":"Dogs: ropinirole (D2 agonist eye drop; reverse with metoclopramide), apomorphine (D2 agonist; compounded), 3% hydrogen peroxide (gastric irritation; fresh each time; most side effects). Cats: hydromorphone (opioid) and low-dose xylazine (α2 agonist). Horses cannot vomit.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":75,"why":"Ropinirole and apomorphine stimulate central dopamine receptors in dogs, triggering the emetic network; hydrogen peroxide instead irritates gastric mucosa and carries aspiration and tissue-injury risk. Cats respond differently to emetics, and alpha-2 agonists or selected opioids may be used under veterinary supervision. Never assume emesis is appropriate after caustics, hydrocarbons, reduced consciousness or an aspiration risk.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-076","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":4,"q":"Prokinetic MOAs for metoclopramide, cisapride, H2 blockers, erythromycin?","answer":"Metoclopramide: D2 antagonist that stimulates gastric emptying. Cisapride: 5-HT4 agonist, so ACh release in the enteric PNS (human form pulled for long QT; now compounded; highly effective). H2 blockers (ranitidine and nizatidine only): anticholinesterase activity raises ACh. Erythromycin (sub-MIC dose): motilin receptor agonist, so ACh release and peristalsis. All prokinetics are contraindicated with GI obstruction.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":76,"why":"Cisapride's 5-HT4 stimulation promotes enteric acetylcholine release, while metoclopramide provides D2 antagonism with upper-GI prokinetic effects. Some H2 blockers also have weak cholinesterase-inhibiting effects, and low-dose erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors. Greater contraction against a physical obstruction increases pressure and injury risk, and cisapride requires attention to QT-prolonging interactions.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-077","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":5,"q":"Prokinetic MOAs for the three equine drugs?","answer":"Lidocaine CRI: blocks Na⁺ channels in the SNS, so PNS activation. Bethanechol: muscarinic agonist. Neostigmine: acetylcholinesterase inhibitor.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":77,"why":"Bethanechol directly stimulates muscarinic receptors, while neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase and increases available acetylcholine at cholinergic synapses. Lidocaine's potential benefit in postoperative equine ileus is more complex than selectively blocking sympathetic nerves, involving neuronal modulation and anti-inflammatory effects. All require appropriate diagnosis and monitoring rather than routine use in obstructive colic.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-078","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":6,"q":"MOAs of the four anti-colic therapies?","answer":"Magnesium sulfate: osmotic agent for impactions (max 3 days in a row). Mineral oil: lubricant that prevents water reabsorption; ascarids, impactions, and a transit marker. Psyllium husk: sand impaction. Butylscopolammonium (Buscopan): muscarinic antagonist that relaxes GI muscle; gas or spasmodic colic and choke.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":78,"why":"Magnesium sulfate retains fluid osmotically in intestinal contents; mineral oil lubricates ingesta, while psyllium's hydrophilic fiber may help manage sand accumulation. Butylscopolammonium blocks muscarinic smooth-muscle contraction, briefly reducing spasms. These address different problems and none substitutes for assessing strangulating or surgical colic; mineral oil absence in feces alone does not establish obstruction.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-079","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":7,"q":"MOAs of the four anti-ulcer therapies, and the hierarchy?","answer":"In order: (1) Proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole oral, pantoprazole IV): irreversibly block the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase; first choice, especially with esophagitis. (2) Sucralfate: sticky cyto-adherent coating that also boosts prostaglandins; stagger from the PPI. (3) Misoprostol: PGE1 agonist (less acid, more bicarbonate and mucus); for NSAID ulcers; never in pregnancy (luteolysis). (4) H2 blockers: reversible; last resort (ranitidine and nizatidine inconsistent, famotidine tolerance in 48 h, cimetidine blocks drug metabolism).","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":79,"why":"Proton-pump inhibitors inhibit parietal H+/K+-ATPase and strongly reduce acid secretion; sucralfate forms a protective barrier over injured mucosa. Misoprostol mimics PGE1, supporting mucus/bicarbonate and decreasing acid but also stimulating uterine activity. H2 blockers competitively reduce histamine-driven acid secretion and can develop tolerance. The lecture's ranking depends on ulcer type and species, not a fixed hierarchy in every case.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-080","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":8,"q":"Three antibiotics for IBD?","answer":"Metronidazole, tylosin, sulfasalazine. Add psyllium during therapy to limit dysbiosis, then probiotics.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":80,"why":"Metronidazole, tylosin and sulfasalazine are historically described for selected chronic intestinal inflammatory conditions, but microbiome disruption and resistance make empirical antibiotic use inappropriate for many cases. Sulfasalazine acts chiefly through local anti-inflammatory activity in the colon. Diet trials, parasite screening, cobalamin status and inflammatory phenotype should guide individualized management.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-081","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":9,"q":"Two general anti-diarrheals and MOAs?","answer":"Bismuth salicylate (secretory diarrhea; salicylate is a COX inhibitor; not in cats) and activated charcoal (sequesters toxins). Kaolin/pectin may bind toxins and bacteria but has weak evidence.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":81,"why":"Bismuth subsalicylate has local protective/antisecretory effects but its salicylate component presents special toxicity concerns in cats. Activated charcoal adsorbs selected ingested toxins in the gut; it is not a routine general treatment for uncomplicated diarrhea and may not bind every toxin. Their mechanisms are different and must be matched to the clinical situation.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-082","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":10,"q":"Why is cobalamin an adjunct for GI disease?","answer":"Chronic bacterial diarrhea (and pancreatic or liver disease) greatly reduces cobalamin absorption, and low cobalamin delays recovery. Give a 6- week SQ course, then recheck levels.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":82,"why":"Cobalamin is needed for methyl-group transfer and cellular metabolism; disease of the ileum, exocrine pancreas or intestinal microbiota can impair uptake and contribute to poor GI recovery. Replacing a deficiency supports tissue function, but a fixed six-week injection regimen is not universally required because oral and injectable approaches depend on patient and monitoring.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-083","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":11,"q":"Anti-pancreatitis drug, how it works, and why it prevents a metabolic disease?","answer":"Fuzapladib (Panoquell-CA1): blocks LFA- 1, which otherwise draws neutrophils into the pancreas and worsens inflammation. Recurrent pancreatitis is a suspected risk factor for diabetes mellitus in dogs, so treating it may protect against DM.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":83,"why":"Fuzapladib inhibits leukocyte function-associated antigen-1 (LFA-1), reducing neutrophil adhesion/migration and pancreatic inflammation in the indicated canine acute-pancreatitis setting. Repeated pancreatic injury may compromise endocrine beta cells, but treating an episode has not established prevention of future diabetes. Do not equate anti-inflammatory action with guaranteed metabolic protection.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-084","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":12,"q":"Two nutraceuticals/botanicals for hepatic dysfunction and how they work?","answer":"SAMe scavenges free radicals that damage hepatocyte membranes. Milk thistle (silybin; combined with SAMe as Denamarin) is hepatoprotective. Both can be tried to prevent death cap mushroom toxicity if given right after ingestion.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":84,"why":"SAMe supports glutathione-related antioxidant defenses; silybin from milk thistle has proposed antioxidant and membrane-protective actions. These are adjunctive supportive products, not proven antidotes that can be relied upon to prevent death-cap mushroom toxicity. Amanita exposure is a medical emergency requiring immediate poison/toxicology management.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-085","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":13,"q":"How do copper chelators, UDA and acetylcysteine help specific liver problems?","answer":"Copper chelators: D-penicillamine promotes urinary copper loss; zinc gluconate blocks copper absorption from the gut. UDA (ursodeoxycholic acid): lowers the harmful chenodeoxycholic acid and promotes bile flow. Acetylcysteine: adjunct for acute liver damage, including acetaminophen toxicosis in cats; also prevents contrast-media nephrotoxicity.","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":85,"why":"D-penicillamine chelates copper to enhance elimination, while zinc reduces intestinal copper absorption. Ursodeoxycholic acid changes bile-acid composition and supports bile flow in selected non-obstructive hepatobiliary disease. N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione in acetaminophen toxicity and some oxidative injuries; routine prevention of contrast nephropathy is not an established universal indication.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-086","lecture":"Lecture 17","section":"GI pharmacology","number":14,"q":"Two drugs for hepatic encephalopathy and how they work?","answer":"Lactulose: not metabolized; gut bacteria acidify the colon, so NH₃ becomes NH₄⁺ and is not absorbed. Rifaximin: unabsorbed antibiotic that reduces urease-positive bacteria, so less gut NH₃. (Metronidazole can be tried but is absorbed.)","note":null,"source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":86,"why":"Gut bacteria generate ammonia from nitrogen compounds; lactulose acidifies colonic contents, trapping diffusible NH3 as poorly absorbed NH4+ and speeding nitrogen elimination. Rifaximin reduces ammonia-producing bacterial activity locally in the intestine. Treating hepatic encephalopathy also requires identifying precipitants such as GI bleeding, constipation, infection or electrolyte abnormalities.","whyProvenance":"Added mechanism/clinical context separate from original 51-page Q&A and 142-page lecture study guide; source answer unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-087","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":1,"q":"Know: what drugs alone can address","answer":"Uncontrollable situational anxieties, compulsive behaviors and cognitive dysfunction can be treated with drugs alone. Everything else: rule out disease first (urine marking and seizures are big clues), then training, with drugs as an adjunct.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Drugs alone rarely address the full behavioral problem. Rule out pain, medical illness and environmental triggers; combine appropriate medication with management, safety planning and behavior modification. The source is an exam framing, not a universal treatment rule.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":87,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-088","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":2,"q":"Which neurotransmitter underlies most behavior problems and is the target of most drugs?","answer":"Serotonin (5-HT), the \"kingpin.\" Priority: 5-HT > dopamine > norepinephrine.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Serotonin is a major pharmacologic target, but aggression, fear, cognitive dysfunction and compulsive behavior are multifactorial; do not assign a single 'kingpin' neurotransmitter as a diagnosis.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":88,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-089","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":3,"q":"Three intervention points for modulating it?","answer":"Enzymes (MAO), reuptake, and receptors. (GABA can only be changed at the receptor.)","note":"The uploaded source combines instructor wording with additional notes; several statements about aggression, serotonin and combinations are oversimplified. Compare with clinical behavior references.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":89},{"id":"exam2-objective-090","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":4,"q":"Why is combination therapy not recommended?","answer":"Transmitter crossover causes side effects (serotonin syndrome risk). Combination only for refractory cases.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Some combinations are appropriate under experienced oversight, while MAOI + SSRI/TCA combinations can cause dangerous serotonergic toxicity. Evaluate actual drugs, doses, interactions and washout rather than forbidding all combination therapy.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":90,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-091","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":5,"q":"Know the general characteristics of the four veterinary-labeled drugs (tradename in parentheses or yellow- highlighted)","answer":"Selegiline (Anipryl): MAO-B inhibitor; cognitive dysfunction in old dogs; monotherapy. Clomipramine (Clomicalm): TCA, blocks serotonin (most selective TCA) and norepinephrine reuptake; dog separation anxiety, compulsive disorders, cat urine marking; pro-drug (not for liver patients), cardiotoxic (not for geriatric, CV disease or glaucoma). Fluoxetine (Reconcile): SSRI; many dog and cat uses with minimal side effects; separation anxiety; cribbing in horses; cat spraying. Mirtazapine (Mirataz): 5-HT antagonist; transdermal gel on the ear for appetite in cats; probably the least cardiotoxic. ⚠ Confirm with him that these are the four he highlighted in yellow.","note":"The uploaded source combines instructor wording with additional notes; several statements about aggression, serotonin and combinations are oversimplified. Compare with clinical behavior references.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":91},{"id":"exam2-objective-092","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":6,"q":"Which drug treats cribbing in horses?","answer":"Fluoxetine (can go in the feed; not bitter).","note":"LECTURE VS CLINIC: The source names fluoxetine for cribbing. Evidence does not establish it as a routine cure; investigate husbandry, welfare, foraging, social contact and medical issues and do not assume serotonin deficiency.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":92,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-093","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":7,"q":"Know: serotonin and aggression","answer":"Most of these drugs raise serotonin, and elevated serotonin is associated with aggression. They are not for treating aggression and can turn an anxious, subservient or compulsive animal aggressive.","note":"CORRECTION: Increased serotonin does NOT universally cause aggression. SSRIs can be used in selected fear/anxiety-associated aggression alongside behavior modification; some animals may show disinhibition or paradoxical agitation. Patient and safety assessment are essential.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":93,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-094","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":8,"q":"What is serotonin syndrome, when is it likely, and how is it treated?","answer":"A toxic buildup of serotonin. Likely with high-dose SSRIs or TCAs, combinations of MAOIs, SSRIs, TCAs and 5-HT agonists (SSRI + trazodone less likely), proserotonergic supplements (St. John's wort, SAMe, tryptophan), tyramine foods with an MAOI (dried meats, nuts, cheese, pickles, bananas), and other drugs (tramadol or any opioid, linezolid, metoclopramide). Treatment: mild = stop the serotonergic drugs and supplements. Severe = cyproheptadine (serotonin receptor blocker), diazepam or phenobarbital for seizures and tremors, fluids, cooling for fever. Survivors look normal in about 36 hours.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Serotonin toxicity combines mental-status change, autonomic instability and neuromuscular hyperactivity after serotonergic exposure. Stop offending agents and provide urgent supportive care; cyproheptadine may be an adjunct, not standalone definitive care. All drug combinations must be assessed individually.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":94,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-095","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying drugs (monoamine modulators)","number":9,"q":"Main cognitive dysfunction drug?","answer":"Selegiline (Anipryl), for geriatric dogs (anecdotally cats). The one to memorize; use as monotherapy.","note":"The uploaded source combines instructor wording with additional notes; several statements about aggression, serotonin and combinations are oversimplified. Compare with clinical behavior references.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":95},{"id":"exam2-objective-096","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying benzodiazepines (BDZs)","number":1,"q":"Know: midazolam","answer":"The only BDZ that MUST be injected. (All others bind soft plastics, so no IV fluids.)","note":"CORRECTION: Midazolam is available in injectable formulations, and other benzodiazepines also have injectable or oral forms depending on product and species. Avoid 'only benzodiazepine that must be injected' as a universal fact; verify route/formulation.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":96,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-097","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying benzodiazepines (BDZs)","number":2,"q":"Know: alprazolam","answer":"Short-acting BDZ for certain anxieties (fear and aggression in dogs, thunderstorm phobia with clomipramine, night anxiety); the most important of the short group.","note":"The uploaded source combines instructor wording with additional notes; several statements about aggression, serotonin and combinations are oversimplified. Compare with clinical behavior references.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":97},{"id":"exam2-objective-098","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying benzodiazepines (BDZs)","number":3,"q":"Know: diazepam","answer":"Long-acting BDZ for certain anxieties and status epilepticus; the most important of the long group.","note":"The uploaded source combines instructor wording with additional notes; several statements about aggression, serotonin and combinations are oversimplified. Compare with clinical behavior references.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":98},{"id":"exam2-objective-099","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying benzodiazepines (BDZs)","number":4,"q":"Why not use diazepam in cats for behavior?","answer":"Risk of fatal idiopathic hepatic necrosis with long-term use. 🎤    One or short-term doses are fine.","note":"CLINICAL CAUTION: Oral diazepam can cause idiosyncratic fulminant hepatic necrosis in cats. Route and exposure matter; do not infer repeated oral dosing is safe from the phrase 'short-term is fine.' Injectable benzodiazepines in an emergency require protocol-specific judgment.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":99,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-100","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying benzodiazepines (BDZs)","number":5,"q":"Know: BDZs are not first choice","answer":"Tolerance and dependence (do not stop abruptly). They are the \"I don't care\" drugs; side effects are sedation, motor problems, and disinhibition aggression in dogs.","note":"The uploaded source combines instructor wording with additional notes; several statements about aggression, serotonin and combinations are oversimplified. Compare with clinical behavior references.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":100},{"id":"exam2-objective-101","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying benzodiazepines (BDZs)","number":6,"q":"Name of the GABA receptor antagonist and when you use it","answer":"Flumazenil: blocks the BDZ site; reverses midazolam cardiovascular effects and BDZ overdoses in any species. It can cause seizures, especially with a TCA or SSRI.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Flumazenil specifically reverses benzodiazepine-site effects, not every cardiovascular cause or other sedative. It can unmask seizures, especially in dependent patients or mixed overdoses; airway support and risk assessment still matter.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":101,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-102","lecture":"Lecture 18","section":"Behavior-modifying benzodiazepines (BDZs)","number":7,"q":"Know: alfaxalone","answer":"A neurosteroid that is an allosteric GABA-A modulator; used as a swine sedative and for anesthesia induction. A poor anticonvulsant because it needs endogenous GABA.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Alfaxalone is a neuroactive steroid GABA-A modulator producing hypnosis/anesthesia, not reliable surgical analgesia. The claim that it is a 'poor anticonvulsant because it requires endogenous GABA' should not be treated as an established stand-alone drug classification.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":102,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-103","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":1,"q":"Two NTs involved in epilepsy?","answer":"GABA (inhibitory) and glutamate (excitatory). Seizures are a synchronous imbalance between them.","note":"The status epilepticus sequence and fixed dosing instructions are not a substitute for current emergency guidelines. Verify primidone and bromide claims.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":103},{"id":"exam2-objective-104","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":2,"q":"Drug of choice for status epilepticus?","answer":"Diazepam (a BDZ) IV every 15 minutes until seizures stop, direct by catheter (it sticks to soft plastic). IM or rectal if the patient is shaking violently. Use midazolam for a CRI. Fine in cats for status epilepticus because use is short- term.","note":"SAFETY: The source's fixed diazepam interval, route and escalating sequence are classroom recall only. Status epilepticus is an emergency; current protocols use patient-specific benzodiazepine rescue, airway/perfusion support and escalation based on response. Do not execute the source sequence as a dosing instruction.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":104,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-105","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":3,"q":"Know: other options if the first choice fails","answer":"In order: diazepam (3–4 doses) → phenobarbital IV → (after 20 minutes) pentobarbital IV (if it works, think strychnine) → propofol CRI. Midazolam (CRI) and levetiracetam are other options. If all fail: ketamine bolus, dexmedetomidine bolus, then CRI of both with mild hypothermia.","note":"SAFETY: The source's precise multi-drug sequence is not a contemporary universal protocol; escalation depends on response, airway support, monitoring and updated status epilepticus guidance. Do not use it as a bedside treatment order.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":105,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-106","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":4,"q":"MOAs of BDZs, barbiturates, levetiracetam","answer":"BDZs: bind the GABA-A receptor and enhance GABA-mediated chloride influx. Barbiturates (phenobarbital, pentobarbital): activate the GABA receptor. Levetiracetam: binds a glutamate vesicle protein and blocks glutamate release. (Propofol activates GABA and blocks the glutamate receptor.)","note":"CLINICAL CLARIFICATION: Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators of GABA-A (increase opening frequency with GABA). Barbiturates prolong channel opening duration and can have additional actions. Levetiracetam binds SV2A; describing it as simply blocking glutamate vesicles is incomplete.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":106,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-107","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":5,"q":"Drug for preventing feline epilepsy?","answer":"Phenobarbital (also the standard for dogs).","note":"The status epilepticus sequence and fixed dosing instructions are not a substitute for current emergency guidelines. Verify primidone and bromide claims.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":107},{"id":"exam2-objective-108","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":6,"q":"Active ingredient in most euthanasia solutions?","answer":"Pentobarbital.","note":"The status epilepticus sequence and fixed dosing instructions are not a substitute for current emergency guidelines. Verify primidone and bromide claims.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":108},{"id":"exam2-objective-109","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":8,"q":"Major side effect in dogs on phenobarbital plus KBr?","answer":"Pancreatitis.","note":"The status epilepticus sequence and fixed dosing instructions are not a substitute for current emergency guidelines. Verify primidone and bromide claims.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":109},{"id":"exam2-objective-110","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (1st study guide)","number":9,"q":"Major side effect in cats on KBr?","answer":"Bronchitis (cough). \"Coughing cat + bromide = bronchitis.\"","note":"The status epilepticus sequence and fixed dosing instructions are not a substitute for current emergency guidelines. Verify primidone and bromide claims.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":110},{"id":"exam2-objective-111","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (2nd study guide)","number":1,"q":"Number-one rule of combination anticonvulsant therapy?","answer":"Never use two drugs with the same MOA (so imepitoin should not be added to phenobarbital, since both are GABA agonists).","note":"CORRECTION: The absolute 'never combine drugs with the same MOA' rule is not universally valid. Rational multidrug epilepsy treatment considers complementary mechanisms, efficacy, interactions, adverse effects and drug levels; regimen decisions are individualized.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":111,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-112","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (2nd study guide)","number":2,"q":"MOAs and uses of levetiracetam and primidone","answer":"Levetiracetam: inhibits glutamate release (glutamate vesicle protein). Effective with few side effects; needs three times daily dosing (once daily in horses); no blood- level monitoring, so good for fractious dogs; a good add-on to phenobarbital or KBr, and an option in status epilepticus. Primidone: a pro-drug converted in the liver to phenobarbital plus phenylethylmalonamide, both anticonvulsant; worth trying in phenobarbital-refractory seizures; will not work with liver impairment; his slide calls it the only drug approved for canine epilepsy. ⚠ Merck lists it as no longer recommended; confirm with him.","note":"CLINICAL CLARIFICATION: Levetiracetam acts at SV2A, modulating presynaptic neurotransmitter release; dosing frequency varies with species, formulation and clinical context. Primidone is metabolized to phenobarbital with additional hepatic monitoring burden and is not interchangeable with levetiracetam.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":112,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-113","lecture":"Lecture 19","section":"Anticonvulsants (2nd study guide)","number":3,"q":"Preferred drug for euthanasia?","answer":"Pentobarbital (small animals). It stays stable in carcasses, so rendering plants refuse them and carnivores can be poisoned. Alternatives: ketamine + xylazine, propofol IV, isoflurane ± KCl.","note":"SAFETY: Humane euthanasia requires appropriate anesthesia/unconsciousness and species-specific legal, personnel and disposal protocols. Alternatives listed in the source are NOT equivalent protocols; KCl is unacceptable in a conscious animal. Pentobarbital residues present scavenger risks.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":113,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-114","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Alpha-2 drugs","number":1,"q":"Know: xylazine","answer":"The preferred sedative in ruminants. Oldest alpha-2 agonist; all major species except swine (no alpha-2 agonist works in swine).","note":"CLINICAL CORRECTION: Alpha-2 agonist responses are species dependent; ruminants often show marked sensitivity and swine may respond inconsistently. The categorical claim that NO alpha-2 agonist works in swine is not supported as a universal statement.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":114,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-115","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Alpha-2 drugs","number":2,"q":"Know: xylazine reversal","answer":"Yohimbine is the preferred reversal agent for xylazine (atipamezole or tolazoline also work).","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":115},{"id":"exam2-objective-116","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Alpha-2 drugs","number":3,"q":"Drug of choice for sedating a dog for allergy skin testing, unusual dosing, preferred antagonist?","answer":"Dexmedetomidine, because it does not interfere with the vasoactive responses to allergens. It is dosed by body surface area (mcg per square meter), not linear mg/kg. Preferred antagonist: atipamezole (yohimbine also works).","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":116},{"id":"exam2-objective-117","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Alpha-2 drugs","number":4,"q":"Know: romifidine","answer":"An equine-specific alpha-2 agonist for colic pain; longest duration of any alpha-2 agonist, very safe, not compatible with other drugs in a syringe.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":117},{"id":"exam2-objective-118","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Alpha-2 drugs","number":5,"q":"Know: medetomidine + vatinoxan","answer":"An alpha-2 agonist/antagonist combination; vatinoxan minimizes the side effects of the agonist.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":118},{"id":"exam2-objective-119","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Phenothiazines","number":1,"q":"Common MOA of phenothiazines?","answer":"Antagonists of the postsynaptic dopamine D2 receptor (like azaperone), giving sedation and tranquilization. Each drug adds more blockade (antihistamine, anticholinergic, anti-adrenergic, anti- serotonergic).","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":119},{"id":"exam2-objective-120","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Phenothiazines","number":2,"q":"Five potential uses of acepromazine?","answer":"(1) Anti-emesis (anti-ACh activity; maropitant is better for long-term use). (2) Exam room sedation and cooperation (can be dispensed before a routine visit; not controlled; avoid before an in-depth CV exam). (3) Travel sedation (airlines do not recommend it; risk of over-sedation and airway obstruction; fine for car rides). (4) Equine laminitis (anti-α1 vasodilation of the digital vessels, given IV). (5) Foal rejection (blocks D2 so prolactin is released, improving maternal bonding). Side effects: hypotension, equine persistent penile prolapse, seizures, poor temperature tolerance; Boxers and Bulldogs especially sensitive.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Acepromazine causes tranquilization, not dependable anxiolysis or analgesia. Alpha-1 blockade can lower BP, and equine penile prolapse is a concern. Do not use immobility as proof that fear or pain has resolved.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":120,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-121","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Phenothiazines","number":3,"q":"Genital side effect of acepromazine and how it helps when buying a horse?","answer":"Persistent penile prolapse in horses (stallions and geldings). When buying one, check for it: it can show up as a result of previous acepromazine use.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":121},{"id":"exam2-objective-122","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Phenothiazines","number":4,"q":"Unique use of cyproheptadine regarding serotonin?","answer":"It is a serotonin receptor blocker used to treat serotonin syndrome.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":122},{"id":"exam2-objective-123","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":1,"q":"Common MOA of opioids?","answer":"They activate opioid receptors (GPCRs: mu, kappa, delta). Postsynaptic activation dampens pain neurons; presynaptic activation blocks GABA release from pain-blocking neurons, which disinhibits them.","note":"CLINICAL CLARIFICATION: Mu/kappa/delta are GPCR opioid targets; presynaptic calcium-channel inhibition and postsynaptic potassium conductance reduce neuronal excitability. Circuit-specific disinhibition can occur, but it is not accurate to treat GABA release inhibition as a universal direction in every pain circuit.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":123,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-124","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":2,"q":"Three most important side effects?","answer":"Respiratory depression, constipation, vomiting.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":124},{"id":"exam2-objective-125","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":3,"q":"Opioid vs opiate?","answer":"Opiates are natural compounds (morphine, codeine). Opioids are synthetic or semi- synthetic derivatives.","note":"CORRECTION: 'Opioid' is the broad term including natural, semisynthetic and synthetic substances that act at opioid receptors. 'Opiate' usually refers to opium-derived natural alkaloids; the source definition excludes natural compounds incorrectly.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":125,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-126","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":4,"q":"Most useful opioid receptor antagonist?","answer":"Naloxone.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":126},{"id":"exam2-objective-127","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":6,"q":"Know how catnip works.","answer":"Nepetalactone appears to be a mu and kappa opioid receptor agonist; the euphoria is an autosomal dominant trait.","note":"CORRECTION: Catnip's nepetalactone activates olfactory sensory pathways; endogenous opioid signaling may contribute to the behavior, but direct nepetalactone mu/kappa receptor agonism should not be asserted as proven.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":127,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-128","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":7,"q":"Know: tramadol","answer":"An effective opioid analgesic in birds, questionable efficacy in dogs (though commonly prescribed for chronic use), and the opioid most likely to cause a seizure because of its effects on catecholamine reuptake channels.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Tramadol has weak mu effects plus serotonergic/noradrenergic actions, with variable efficacy in dogs and potential seizure/interaction risks. Avoid universally ranking it as the 'most' seizurogenic opioid or extrapolating between species.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":128,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-129","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":8,"q":"Know: butorphanol","answer":"A mixed agonist-antagonist: agonist at the kappa receptor, antagonist at mu. For mild-to- moderate pain (soft tissue, GI surgery); less respiratory depression than morphine. Very good for visceral pain in horses, does not appreciably inhibit gut motility, and is not excitatory in horses or cats. The mu antagonism lowers side effects and dependence, so it can be used longer post- operatively than morphine, and it can prevent wind-up. Sedative dose is lower than the analgesic dose. IV is best (IM and SQ irritate); oral works as an antitussive.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Butorphanol has mixed opioid actions, generally limited analgesic duration and ceiling effects; it is often valued for antitussive activity and selected equine visceral pain contexts. It is not adequate sole analgesia for many painful surgeries.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":129,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-130","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":9,"q":"Know: buprenorphine","answer":"A mu agonist (partial) and kappa antagonist; more potent than morphine, less dependence because of the kappa antagonism, and less respiratory depression (mu1 but not mu2 agonist). Approved in cats as 3 daily injections or a transdermal gel that lasts 4 days.","note":"CLINICAL DISTINCTION: Buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial mu agonist with formulation- and route-specific duration. 'More potent' does not mean greater maximal efficacy; do not generalize exact FDA-approved formulations or intervals between cats, products or regions.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":130,"clinicalReviewStatus":"Targeted source-versus-clinical clarification Nov 17 2026; original professor-derived answer remains unchanged."},{"id":"exam2-objective-131","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":10,"q":"Know: hydrocodone","answer":"An anti-tussive: mu activity suppresses the central cough center. The syrup contains homatropine, an anticholinergic that reduces respiratory secretions and also reduces the chance of abuse. Excellent bioavailability in dogs; prescribed for kennel cough.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":131},{"id":"exam2-objective-132","lecture":"Lecture 20","section":"Opioids","number":11,"q":"How to prevent wind-up?","answer":"Pre-emptive analgesia: give an opioid before (and after) surgery. Wind-up is NMDA-driven central sensitization after a painful event that can turn into chronic pain.","note":"Species-generalized alpha-2 claims, acepromazine seizure effects and opioid receptor/pharmacology statements require current clinical review.","source":"Uploaded Pharmacology Exam 2: Study Guide Answers and Practice Questions, Part 1 (Nov 14, 2026)","order":132}];
